.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/package-json-from-dist Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "package-json-from-dist"
.PP
Sometimes you want to load the \fRpackage\&.json\fP into your TypeScript program, and it's tempting to just \fRimport '\&.\&./package\&.json'\fP, since that seems to work\&.

.PP
However, this requires \fRtsc\fP to make an entire copy of your \fRpackage\&.json\fP file into the \fRdist\fP folder, which is a problem if you're using something like \fRtshy\fP, which uses the \fRpackage\&.json\fP file in dist for another purpose\&. Even when that does work, it's asking the module system to do a bunch of extra fs system calls, just to load a version number or something\&. (See \fRthis issue\fP\&.)

.PP
This module helps by just finding the package\&.json file appropriately, and reading and parsing it in the most normal fashion\&.
.SH "Caveats"
.PP
This \fIonly\fP works if your code builds into a target folder called \fRdist\fP, which is in the root of the package\&. It also requires that you do not have a folder named \fRnode_modules\fP anywhere within your dev environment, or else it'll get the wrong answers there\&. (But, at least, that'll be in dev, so you're pretty likely to notice\&.)

.PP
If you build to some other location, then you'll need a different approach\&. (Feel free to fork this module and make it your own, or just put the code right inline, there's not much of it\&.)
.SH "USAGE"
.PP
.PP
.nf
// src/index\&.ts
import {
  findPackageJson,
  loadPackageJson,
} from 'package\-json\-from\-dist'

const pj = findPackageJson(import\&.meta\&.url)
console\&.log(`package\&.json found at ${pj}`)

const pkg = loadPackageJson(import\&.meta\&.url)
console\&.log(`Hello from ${pkg\&.name}@${pkg\&.version}`)
.fi
.PP

.PP
If your module is not directly in the \fR\&./src\fP folder, then you need to specify the path that you would expect to find the \fRpackage\&.json\fP when it's \fInot\fP built to the \fRdist\fP folder\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// src/components/something\&.ts
import {
  findPackageJson,
  loadPackageJson,
} from 'package\-json\-from\-dist'

const pj = findPackageJson(import\&.meta\&.url, '\&.\&./\&.\&./package\&.json')
console\&.log(`package\&.json found at ${pj}`)

const pkg = loadPackageJson(import\&.meta\&.url, '\&.\&./\&.\&./package\&.json')
console\&.log(`Hello from ${pkg\&.name}@${pkg\&.version}`)
.fi
.PP

.PP
When running from CommmonJS, use \fR__filename\fP instead of \fRimport\&.meta\&.url\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// src/index\&.cts
import {
  findPackageJson,
  loadPackageJson,
} from 'package\-json\-from\-dist'

const pj = findPackageJson(__filename)
console\&.log(`package\&.json found at ${pj}`)

const pkg = loadPackageJson(__filename)
console\&.log(`Hello from ${pkg\&.name}@${pkg\&.version}`)
.fi
.PP

.PP
Since \fRtshy\fP builds \fIboth\fP CommonJS and ESM by default, you may find that you need a CommonJS override and some \fR//@ts-ignore\fP magic to make it work\&.

.PP
\fRsrc/pkg\&.ts\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import {
  findPackageJson,
  loadPackageJson,
} from 'package\-json\-from\-dist'
//@ts\-ignore
export const pkg = loadPackageJson(import\&.meta\&.url)
//@ts\-ignore
export const pj = findPackageJson(import\&.meta\&.url)
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fRsrc/pkg-cjs\&.cts\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import {
  findPackageJson,
  loadPackageJson,
} from 'package\-json\-from\-dist'
export const pkg = loadPackageJson(__filename)
export const pj = findPackageJson(__filename)
.fi
.PP
 
